1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,420
Can empty space make something from nothing?

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:05,815
Empty space is never perfectly still.

3
00:00:05,815 --> 00:00:09,180
Quantum physics says its energy is always jittering.

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:12,863
That jitter lets a particle and its antiparticle

5
00:00:12,863 --> 00:00:15,433
borrow a little energy and pop into existence,

6
00:00:15,683 --> 00:00:16,801
just for an instant.

7
00:00:16,801 --> 00:00:18,310
Then they meet, and vanish.

8
00:00:19,310 --> 00:00:21,893
But at the edge of a black hole, one of them can fall in,

9
00:00:22,093 --> 00:00:23,180
while the other escapes.

10
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:26,380
And that escaping glow is real.

11
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:29,857
It's called Hawking radiation, predicted in 1974,

12
00:00:29,857 --> 00:00:32,270
though nobody has detected it yet.

13
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:34,954
So who pays back the borrowed energy?

14
00:00:35,354 --> 00:00:37,130
The black hole does, with its own mass.

15
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:43,090
A black hole as heavy as the Sun would need 10⁶⁷ years to evaporate.

16
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:46,600
The universe is only 13.8 billion years old.

17
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,320
So even at the edge of a black hole, nothing is ever truly free.

